# 📑 1. Project Overview

HireSwift AI is an intelligent Applicant Tracking System designed to help recruiters manage recruitment activities, extract candidate information from resumes, analyze job descriptions, evaluate candidate suitability, and manage the hiring pipeline.

The application provides resume ingestion, structured candidate extraction, deterministic candidate scoring, Gemini-powered AI explanations, candidate ranking, candidate comparison, skill gap analysis, hiring pipeline management, email drafting, and recruitment analytics.

This testing specification is intended to validate the actual implementation in the supplied HireSwift AI source code.

## 1.1 Primary Testing Objectives

- Verify that the application launches and renders correctly.
- Validate all navigation links and workspace modules.
- Verify PDF and DOCX resume uploads.
- Test resume extraction and candidate profile generation.
- Verify Gemini integration and deterministic fallback processing.
- Validate job description creation and AI analysis.
- Verify candidate matching and weighted scoring calculations.
- Test candidate filtering, searching, sorting, and pagination.
- Validate duplicate detection and duplicate resolution.
- Test hiring pipeline transitions and recruiter notes.
- Verify candidate comparison and skill gap analysis.
- Validate email templates and export functionality.
- Test dashboard analytics and reports.
- Verify browser persistence and data reset behavior.
- Test backend REST API endpoints and error handling.
- Test responsive layouts and browser compatibility.
- Identify crashes, incorrect calculations, missing functionality, and misleading AI output.

## 1.2 Testing Philosophy

Testing must be performed against the existing source code.

Do not assume that a feature works because it is listed in the README.

Every feature must be tested through its actual user interface, application logic, or API endpoint.

Classify every feature using one of these statuses:

- PASS – Works according to the requirement.
- FAIL – Does not work or produces incorrect results.
- BLOCKED – Cannot be tested because a prerequisite is unavailable.
- PARTIAL – Some expected behavior works, but other behavior is incomplete.
- NOT IMPLEMENTED – The feature does not exist in the supplied implementation.
- NOT APPLICABLE – The test does not apply to the current architecture.

Never mark a test as PASS without executing it and recording evidence.

